#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM7030", unit: "U1")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U1.1 Category axioms",[Verify that nonnegative integers with an arrow $m -> n$ exactly when $m<=n$ form a category.],[Use reflexivity and transitivity.],[There is at most one arrow of each type.],[Reflexivity supplies identities; transitivity supplies a composite whenever arrows are composable. Both possible associative composites have the same source and target and hence are the unique arrow of that type. Identity laws hold for the same reason.],[Check identities, existence of composition, and associativity.]),
 p(2,"E","U1.1 One-object categories",[Describe the one-object category associated with the additive monoid $NN$. What arrows are invertible?],[Composition is addition and identity is zero.],[An inverse would be a nonnegative integer adding to zero.],[There is one object, arrows are nonnegative integers, composition is addition, and the identity arrow is zero. Only zero is invertible, since $n+m=0$ with both nonnegative forces both zero.],[Distinguish the object from its many endomorphisms.]),
 p(3,"E","U1.2 Opposites",[Describe the opposite of a poset category.],[Reverse the order relation.],[An arrow $a -> b$ now means $b<=a$.],[The opposite has the same objects with the reversed order. Identities are unchanged, and transitivity of the reversed order supplies composition.],[State the new arrow condition explicitly.]),
 p(4,"E","U1.1 Isomorphisms",[Prove an isomorphism in the category of groups is exactly a bijective group homomorphism.],[The inverse of a bijection must also preserve the operation.],[Apply the original homomorphism to the desired inverse equation.],[An inverse morphism makes the underlying function bijective. Conversely let $f:G -> H$ be a bijective homomorphism. For $x=f(a),y=f(b)$, $f^(-1)(x y)=f^(-1)(f(a b))=a b=f^(-1)(x)f^(-1)(y)$. Thus its inverse function is a homomorphism.],[Prove the reverse direction rather than treating inverse preservation as automatic.]),
 p(5,"C","U1.2 Monomorphisms",[Prove the composite of two monomorphisms is monic.],[Cancel one factor at a time.],[From $n m u=n m v$, first cancel $n$.],[If $n,m$ are monic and $(n m)u=(n m)v$, monicity of $n$ gives $m u=m v$, then monicity of $m$ gives $u=v$. This is the definition of monicity of the composite.],[State the sources and targets needed for all composites.]),
 p(6,"C","U1.2 Cancellation",[If $g f$ is monic, prove $f$ is monic. Must $g$ be monic?],[Precompose both sides of an equality with $g$.],[For the counterexample use an inclusion followed by a constant map on a larger set.],[If $f u=f v$, then $g f u=g f v$, so monicity of $g f$ gives $u=v$. The second claim fails: let $f$ include a singleton into a two-element set and let $g$ be the constant map to a singleton. The composite is a bijection on a singleton, hence monic, but $g$ is not injective.],[Prove the implication and supply a fully typed counterexample.]),
 p(7,"C","U1.2 Split arrows",[Show a morphism with a left inverse is monic. Give a monomorphism of sets with no left inverse.],[Use cancellation for the proof; consider the empty set for the example.],[The map from the empty set to a singleton is injective.],[If $r f=1$, then $f u=f v$ implies $u=r f u=r f v=v$. The unique map $emptyset -> 1$ is injective and therefore monic, but it has no left inverse because no map $1 -> emptyset$ exists.],[A left inverse must be an actual arrow with the required source.]),
 p(8,"C","U1.2 Epimorphisms",[Prove a function missing an element of its codomain is not epic in sets.],[Construct two functions from the codomain to a two-element set.],[Make them differ only at the missing element.],[If $f:X -> Y$ misses $y_0$, let $u:Y -> \{0,1\}$ be constantly zero and let $v$ be one at $y_0$ and zero elsewhere. Then $u!=v$ but $u f=v f$, because $f$ never reaches $y_0$. Hence $f$ fails the epi cancellation property.],[Specify the test maps and why their composites agree.]),
 p(9,"C","U1.1 Groupoids",[Show the isomorphisms in any category, retaining all objects, form a subcategory in which every arrow is invertible.],[Check identities and composition.],[The inverse of $g f$ is $f^(-1)g^(-1)$.],[Identities are isomorphisms. For isomorphisms $f:A->B$ and $g:B->C$, the composite $g f$ has proposed inverse $f^(-1)g^(-1)$. It satisfies both inverse equations by associativity, so composites of isomorphisms remain isomorphisms. The inverses themselves are isomorphisms, hence lie in this subcategory. It is a groupoid.],[Verify the composition order.]),
 p(10,"A","U1.2 Balancedness",[Prove a morphism that is both monic and split epic is an isomorphism.],[Let $f s=1$.],[Compare $f(s f)$ with $f$.],[Let $f:A->B$ and choose its right inverse $s:B->A$. Since $f s=1_B$, we have $f(s f)=(f s)f=f=f 1_A$. Monicity of $f$ gives $s f=1_A$. Thus $s$ is both a right and left inverse, and $f$ is an isomorphism.],[Do not assume an arbitrary epi has a section.]),
 p(11,"A","U1.2 Rings versus sets",[Prove the inclusion $ZZ -> ZZ[1/2]$ is epic among commutative unital rings but is not an isomorphism.],[A map must send $1/2$ to the inverse of two.],[Inverses, when they exist, are unique.],[If two homomorphisms out of $ZZ[1/2]$ agree on $ZZ$, their values on $1/2$ are both inverses of the common image of two, hence agree. Every element is $a/2^n$, so all their values agree. This proves the inclusion epic. It is not onto because $1/2$ is not an integer, and a ring isomorphism must be a bijection.],[Quantify over every possible target ring and compare all fractions.]),
 p(12,"A","U1.1 Categories from paths",[Construct the free category on a directed graph and prove any assignment of its vertices and edges to objects and arrows of a category extends uniquely to a functor.],[Use finite composable paths, including empty paths.],[A functor must send a path to the composite of its edge images.],[Objects are vertices; arrows are finite composable paths; empty paths are identities; composition concatenates paths. Concatenation is associative. Given a graph assignment preserving endpoints, send each path to the composite of its edge images and each empty path to an identity. Associativity and identity laws in the target prove this is a functor. Every path is a composite of edges, so any extension must have these values, proving uniqueness.],[Include empty paths and explain why no additional path relations are imposed.]),
)
